docs/research/connections/sahely-grammar-of-violence.md:5:title: "Sahely (2026-02-24) The Grammar of Violence ↔ Spore trap-shape vocabulary + life-value doctrine + structural-legitimacy"
docs/research/connections/sahely-grammar-of-violence.md:37:# Sahely (2026-02-24) The Grammar of Violence ↔ Spore trap-shape vocabulary + life-value doctrine + structural-legitimacy
docs/research/connections/sahely-grammar-of-violence.md:39:**Caveat (read first):** This is a Spore-side substrate-resonance bridge note for Bichara Sahely's 2026-02-24 white paper *"The Grammar of Violence: Structural Drivers of Systemic Harm and Pathways to Viability"* (46 pages; bsahely.com blog post + companion PDF; AI-co-authored with ChatGPT 5.2 + NotebookLM per cover byline). It is a **descriptive Layer-1.5 comparative-intake artifact** and is **framing-note-only**: it surfaces no admission proposal. The relevant canon-pressure this post engages was already dispositioned in the Sahely-corpus Bundle α arc (ADRs 0085–0090, landed 2026-05-23 → 05-29; concepts-yaml v24; 40 canon-decisions; 4 cross-cutting doctrines). This note cites the **resolved** state of that canon — not the Phase-2 "pending DECISION-BRIEF" framing. Per single-source-rigor discipline, Sahely is one AI-co-authored author corpus within a Galtung+McMurtry primary-source lineage; cross-post recurrence within his corpus is **not** independent evidence and this note does not claim cluster-counting convergence. Cross-repo §5 is descriptive comparison only per stream-scope discipline; it makes no write-side recommendation.
docs/research/connections/sahely-grammar-of-violence.md:138:### 4.4 Grammar-determines-the-legitimate + Galtung structural violence ↔ perception-as-power (ADR-0090)
docs/research/connections/sahely-grammar-of-violence.md:163:Sahely's 2026-02-24 *Grammar of Violence* is an integrative synthesis (Galtung 1990/1996/2013 + McMurtry 2003/2014) diagnosing war, ecological collapse, financial volatility, and public-sector erosion as homologous outputs of one layered architecture: cultural narratives → institutional design → structural outcomes → direct violence → reinforcement (C-7). Read against Spore's **post-Bundle-α** canon, every theme maps to **already-resolved** canon:
tmp/canon-coherence-falsifiability-audit-2026-04-22.md:517:- **Rationale**: Opus-4-7 §3.11's call. Two honest routes: (1) commit substantively to the Ostrom-commons + Alexander-pattern-language + multiple-protocols definitions, which would require major new governance specifications and pattern-language restructuring; (2) rename to something that doesn't make these technical claims ("Agent Coordination Infrastructure" / "Sovereign Coordination Grammar"). Pre-coherence-audit this was unactionable; post-coherence-audit it's still heavier than Phase 2-3 repairs and needs its own plan. Hold for Phase 4+ after missing-foundations are authored.
tmp/canon-coherence-falsifiability-audit-2026-04-22.md:549:**Canon and meta-canon have different evidence profiles.** Opus-4-7 §4 is correct: the self-governance machinery (canon-review protocol, learning membrane, bridge-notes, dispositions, ADRs, held-tensions) is richly developed and battle-tested; the object-level coordination grammar is thinner. This audit's table reflects the asymmetry — the methodological-commitments (C-22, C-23) are among the strongest items; the primitive-claims are among the weakest. Honest canon positioning would acknowledge: Spore has built a way of producing coordination vocabularies more than (yet) a definitive coordination vocabulary.
docs/research/connections/institutions-and-extitutions.md:114:The cluster's central thesis: in the wiki's institutional-theory corpus, **extitutional is not the contrary of institutional** (Schingler/de Filippi, Extitutions.org) — the two are complementary lenses onto the same social phenomena. Spore's multi-actor coordination grammar, which composes with both formal institutions (organizations, contracts, protocols) and extitutional processes (rhizomatic assemblies, network-form coordination, citizen-driven self-empowerment), should state this composition discipline explicitly rather than inheriting either the anti-institutional or pro-institutional valence by default.
docs/research/connections/institutions-and-extitutions.md:144:Spicer's framing is structurally dialectical: institutions and extitutions are not two separate things but the two poles of an ongoing tension, where institutional work is the containment of the extitutional excess. This adds a failure-mode reading to the neutral lens-complementarity framing: coordination grammars that import only the institutional lens will understate the extitutional overflow (soft governance failure); grammars that import only the extitutional lens will understate the containment work institutions perform (hard governance failure).
docs/research/connections/institutions-and-extitutions.md:150:This pairs with the extitutional-processes framing to give Spore a two-sided discipline: (a) extitutional processes exist and operate in a scale-limited, dynamic, mutating register; (b) at scale-over-time, institutional crystallization is structural, not optional. Spore's coordination grammar at bioregional scale cannot assume indefinite extitutional fluidity.
docs/research/connections/institutions-and-extitutions.md:162:Douglas Allen's `Institutional Revolution` adds a complementary empirical frame: institutional change in early-modern England was driven by the ability to *measure* performance. "[P]rogress has been often little more than the removal of randomness in outcomes." Allen's insight is that new measurement capacities open new institutional possibilities — a claim that is directly relevant to any coordination grammar that proposes new measurement primitives (per Spore/IC/PM discussions of evidence capture, commitment records, claim registers) as drivers of institutional innovation.
docs/research/connections/institutions-and-extitutions.md:217:Spicer's dialectical reading — "institutions are driven by a tension between attempts to create conformity to modern rational myths and attempts to escape or resist these myths in various ways … we can look at an institution as a huge machine designed to regulate and contain these extitutional elements, whereby the work of institutions is precisely this work of containment" — adds a failure-mode register to the neutral lens-complementarity framing: coordination grammars that import only the extitutional lens understate the containment work institutions perform, and grammars that import only the institutional lens understate the extitutional overflow.
docs/research/connections/institutions-and-extitutions.md:229:Roberto Unger's institutional-experimentalism framing names "motivated, directed, and cumulative experimentation with the institutional forms that now define representative democracy, market economy, and free civil society" and argues that "the reinvention of structure ordinarily takes place step-by-step and part-by-part" — treating institutions as iterative-design objects rather than givens and supplying a stronger-than-rhetorical register for Spore claims that coordination grammars are objects of experimentation.
docs/research/connections/institutions-and-extitutions.md:235:Lars Zimmermann's institutions-of-open-source typology specifies concrete institutional instruments — READ ME files, licenses, version-control systems, foundations, standards — as load-bearing institutional infrastructure for open-source coordination, and notes that "insecurities indicate best a lack of working institutions" (on open-source hardware licensing); this supplies an operational test for institutional presence — the presence of widely-adopted concrete instruments, not the absence of hierarchy — that Spore and IC can use to assess whether their coordination grammars carry the institutional surface they claim.
docs/research/connections/bkc-as-instance-family.md:57:BKC is already Spore-downstream. The 2026-04-08 BKC refresh audit (`BioregionalKnowledgeCommoning/docs/research/bkc-refresh-audit-2026-04-08.md`) ratified the boundary explicitly: BKC is downstream from Spore for coordination grammar, downstream from IC for intelligence primitives, and primary for bioregional application + reference profiles + pilots + proving-ground. That memo recommended a 5-step refresh-pass that has not yet been executed; the BKC corpus continues to operate against the pre-canon-rebuild slug surface (one stable Spore citation: `spore.mycelial-holarchy-architecture`, the doc_id of `holonic-network-architecture.md` which Spore preserved through the canon-rebuild arc).
