docs/research/connections/what-the-commons-is-missing-opposition.md:61:This is a sharp framing for Spore/IC/PM canon: any coordination grammar that uses market-adjacent language (matching, routing, exchange, pooling) without specifying whether participants are market-*enabled* (can use the market when they want) or market-*dependent* (must use the market regardless of preference) is taking a political position by silence. Wood's framing does not demand any of the three projects adopt a specific position on market-dependence — it demands that any of them invoking market-adjacent language state *which* position they are taking. Silence defaults to reproducing market imperatives, which collapses the distinction from capitalism proper.
docs/research/connections/what-the-commons-is-missing-opposition.md:106:Dietz's reactionary-framing critique — "too much is tied implicitly or explicitly to Marx, and there are far too few references to spirituality, aesthetics, positive health, and the great philosophical traditions of the non-Western world ... this often seems to make the movement at times more reactionary, in the sense of simply throwing off existing incompetent overlords, rather than casting a positive vision for the future" — names a specific failure mode (anti-oppressor framing without positive-vision specification) that applies to coordination grammars that define themselves primarily against extant institutions without specifying what the grammar is positively for.
docs/research/connections/what-the-commons-is-missing-opposition.md:112:Ellen Meiksins Wood's market-dependence framing (Monthly Review 1999) distinguishes "market opportunities and market imperatives, or market-enablement and market-dependence" as a politically-consequential distinction — establishing that any coordination grammar invoking market-adjacent language (matching, routing, exchange, pooling) must specify which of the two positions it occupies, and that silence on the distinction defaults to reproducing market imperatives by omission.
docs/research/connections/what-the-commons-is-missing-opposition.md:124:The social-solidarity-economy framing (Conaty/Lewis, via John Pearce's third-system typology) specifies reciprocity as "the central economic principle that shapes the management of markets, trade, and capital" — making reciprocity a measurable, position-bearing principle rather than a rhetorical stance, and establishing that any coordination grammar invoking commons lineage must specify how reciprocity-measurement and the three-system composition (private / public / third-system) are operationalized, not only named.
docs/research/connections/what-the-commons-is-missing-opposition.md:136:The P2P Foundation wiki's Category:Commons Economics corpus-level framing — "Commons Economics doesn't start with the scarcity paradigm, but with maintaining and growing abundance through mutualization and sharing resources, and it looks systematically for win-win strategies" — establishes that the commons-economics tradition the Spore/IC/PM learning field draws on has an explicit *starting* discipline (abundance/mutualization, not scarcity/competition) that coordination grammars invoking commons lineage must either adopt or explicitly decline; silent invocation without the starting discipline replicates the neoclassical-neoliberal assumption the category explicitly rejects.
docs/research/connections/what-the-commons-is-missing-opposition.md:139:The corpus-breadth evidence at Category:Commons Economics positions commons-based economics as requiring specific discipline — "What pricing is for the market, and planning is for state-driven economies, so is mutual coordination for commons economics" — making mutual coordination a category-defining operation rather than a decorative addition; coordination grammars invoking commons lineage without the mutual-coordination operational surface (beyond the term itself) fail the category's own test for belonging to the commons-economics tradition rather than merely borrowing its vocabulary.
docs/research/corpus-review/originals/sahely-emails/2026-05-27-living-coherence-and-neuropsychiatric-disease-autopoiesis-structural-coupling.md:56:    url: https://bsahely.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Applying_the_Seven_Primitives_Clinical_Grammar.mp3
docs/research/corpus-review/originals/sahely-emails/2026-05-27-living-coherence-and-neuropsychiatric-disease-autopoiesis-structural-coupling.md:57:    label: "Applying the Seven Primitives Clinical Grammar"
docs/research/corpus-review/originals/sahely-emails/2026-05-27-living-coherence-and-neuropsychiatric-disease-autopoiesis-structural-coupling.md:97:The original contribution of the paper is the Seven-Primitives Clinical Grammar: Constraint, Margin, State, Disturbance, Perception, Regulation, and Options. These primitives allow clinicians, researchers, and policy designers to ask: What is blocked? Where has reserve capacity been depleted? What is the present bodily-affective-relational state? What disturbances are acting? What is being made salient? What regulatory strategies are being used? What viable paths can be reopened? This grammar provides a transdiagnostic formulation method that complements existing systems while restoring life-process coherence to the center of psychiatric understanding.
tmp/canon-first-principles-audit-2026-04-22.md:37:**Claim**: this six-element ecology is the canonical coordination grammar, applicable across scales.
tmp/canon-first-principles-audit-2026-04-22.md:48:- **Option A (modest)**: Demote from "the coordination ecology" to "one common vocabulary for discovery-phase coordination under complexity." Explicitly name other coordination grammars (improvisational, ritual, embedded, emergent) and decline to privilege one.
docs/research/connections/johar-speech-and-duty.md:43:The essay frames society as a collective intelligence system trained on its own outputs. This resonates with Spore's coordination grammar: the grammar IS a society-level intelligence architecture. But the essay's frame is broader — it applies to the entire public sphere, not just to governed knowledge systems. The implication for Spore is that the grammar's principles (revisability, evidence-linking, staged promotion, confidence-grading) are not just internal governance mechanisms but models for civic-scale epistemic infrastructure.
docs/research/connections/sahely-self-as-viability-stack.md:23:    pages: "1-11 (cover + TOC + abstract/keywords + executive summary + §1 Why the Self Still Fragments + §2 Grammar of Viability as Hidden Unifier; pp 12-52 deferred per extraction record)"
docs/research/connections/sahely-self-as-viability-stack.md:29:**Caveat (read first):** This is a Spore-side substrate-resonance bridge note for Bichara Sahely's 2026-02-10 white paper *"The Self as a Viability Stack: From Mitochondrial Proto-Subjectivity to Narrative Identity"* (52 pages; bsahely.com blog post + companion PDF; AI-co-authored with ChatGPT 5.2 as AI Research Partner). It is a **descriptive** comparative-intake artifact at Layer 1.5 of the learning-membrane intake program (`feedback_intake_to_vocab_admission_program.md`): a Phase-3 framing-note that surfaces cross-tradition resonance against Spore's post-Bundle-α canon. It is **framing-note-only** — it admits nothing. Per honest-rigor single-source discipline, Sahely is one AI-co-authored author corpus; cross-post recurrence within his corpus is **not independent evidence**, and this note does not sum convergence. Cross-repo §5 is descriptive comparison only per stream-scope discipline (`feedback_workstream_scope_discipline.md`); it makes no write-side recommendation at IC/bregion/BKC/PM.
docs/research/connections/sahely-self-as-viability-stack.md:42:- **Extraction depth:** Phase-1 read covered pp 1-11 (cover + TOC + abstract/keywords + executive summary + §1 + §2), producing 12 verbatim C-claims plus a four-layer viability-stack table. Pages 12-52 (Chapters 3-11 on Damasio, mitochondria, brainstem integration, trauma/self-fragmentation, traditional-medicine mappings; Appendices A-H; References) are **not** read at extraction time. This note relies on the pp 1-11 substrate and makes no claims about chapter-internal moves requiring deeper reading.
docs/research/connections/sahely-self-as-viability-stack.md:46:The extraction record preserves `ai_co_authored: true` with `ai_tool_credits: ["ChatGPT 5.2 (AI Research Partner)"]` and `injection_signal_detected: false`. The substantive framing decisions — anchoring the proto-self in mitochondrial psychobiology, the four-layer viability-stack architecture, the "felt enforcement of viability constraints" reading of consciousness, the trauma-as-interface-injury reframe — are Sahely's, situated within the Damasio / Panksepp / Solms / Picard affective-neuroscience lineage that pre-dates LLM availability. This note cites C-claims as Sahely's articulation under that disclosure; it does not treat AI-drafting scaffolding as a defeater of attribution.
docs/research/connections/sahely-self-as-viability-stack.md:144:Sahely's 2026-02-10 *The Self as a Viability Stack* reframes Damasio's proto-self / core-self / autobiographical-self typology as a four-layer architecture of viability-regulating interfaces, anchored in mitochondrial proto-subjectivity, with consciousness as the *felt enforcement of viability constraints* and trauma as *interface injury*. Read against Spore's post-Bundle-α canon, it produces **six framing-note resonances** and **zero canon-pressure**:
