docs/research/connections/faircoin-filtering-membrane-opposition.md:144:- **R5**: Spore's `mycorrhizal-federation-protocol` MUST NOT use federation-filtering framing (selective admission of peers, selective propagation of signals, membrane-at-the-edge) without concurrently specifying how the Dallyn-Frenzel failure modes are prevented at federation scale — specifically: (a) how node-membership boundaries are specified and verified (not merely vouched-for); (b) how federation-level capital or value flows (if any) are bounded from external-market valuation dynamics; (c) how peer-to-peer reputational feedback governs access to federation-level scarce resources; (d) how accounting transparency is maintained across federation nodes. Absent these specifications, federation-level filtering framing inherits the FairCoin failure surface at a higher power-concentration scale, where the failure would be correspondingly harder to recover from (arbitrage and convertibility failures at federation scale produce power-capture dynamics, not only capital-drainage). Opposition edge retires when the protocol doc independently addresses all four surfaces, or explicitly declines federation-filtering framing in favor of an alternative coordination grammar. [target: spore.mycorrhizal-federation-protocol] [concept: power-capture]
docs/research/connections/sahely-end-of-kings.md:31:**Caveat (read first):** This is a Spore-side substrate-resonance bridge note for a **foundation-era (2018-09-04)** Bichara Sahely blog post that **reposts a Sustainable Human / Caitlin Johnstone narration** titled *"The End of Kings."* It is a **descriptive, framing-note-only** comparative-intake artifact at Layer 1.5 of the intake-to-vocab-admission program (`feedback_intake_to_vocab_admission_program.md`). It surfaces resonances against the **resolved** post-Bundle-α Spore canon (concepts-yaml v24; 4 cross-cutting doctrines; 40 canon-decisions through ADR-0090); it does **not** propose any admission. This is the **pre-AI-collaboration era** of the corpus (`ai_co_authored: false`) — well before the Dec-2022+ AI-co-authored white-paper explosion — and **single-source rigor** applies twice over: (a) this is one author's *curation*, not original authorship (REPOST), and (b) even within Sahely's broader corpus, cross-post recurrence is **not** independent evidence and is not treated as convergence. Cross-repo §5 is descriptive comparison only per stream-scope discipline (`feedback_workstream_scope_discipline.md`); it makes no write-side recommendation.
docs/research/connections/sahely-end-of-kings.md:121:C10–C12 turn from diagnosis to emancipation: shared information dissolves the kings' power; the people *collectively change their minds about how money works*; and a "humanity that works in collaboration with itself and with its ecosystem" emerges. The agency-from-below register — situational capacity emerging in collective response — is what `constructed-power` and `expressive-power` name (ADR-0048). The end-state ("collaboration with itself and with its ecosystem") brushes `civil-commons` (ADR-0087), the life-enabling commons-structure that is the constructive counter-pole to extractive domination. Reinforcing, descriptive; no canon-shift. (Per `feedback_surface_vocabulary_vs_operational_concern.md`, these are surface-vocabulary brushes onto Spore's agency vocabulary, not operational-concern identity — a political allegory is not an operational coordination grammar.)
docs/research/connections/sahely-end-of-kings.md:136:- **PM** (Poietic Match): no strong engagement; the allegory is a power diagnosis, not a matchmaking/coordination grammar. Descriptive only.
