docs/research/connections/sahely-grammar-of-viability.md:46:**Caveat (read first):** This is a Spore-side substrate-resonance bridge note for Bichara Sahely's 2026-02-11 book-length work *"The Grammar of Viability: Mind, Self, Meaning, and the Conditions of Enduring Life — Diagnosing Fragmentation, Recovering Orientation, and Keeping Futures Open"* (53-page PDF; AI-co-authored with ChatGPT-5). It is a **descriptive** Layer-1.5 comparative-intake artifact (`feedback_intake_to_vocab_admission_program.md`): it surfaces how the post's themes resonate against Spore's *already-resolved* post-Bundle-α canon (concepts-yaml v24; 4 cross-cutting doctrines; 40 canon-decisions through ADR-0090). It is **framing-note-only throughout** — it proposes no admission. Cross-repo §5 is descriptive comparison only per stream-scope discipline (`feedback_workstream_scope_discipline.md`); it makes no write-side recommendation for IC / bregion / BKC / PM.
docs/research/connections/sahely-grammar-of-viability.md:75:- **C-3** [anchor: §Abstract · orn:source:bsahely-2026-02-11-the-grammar-of-viability-mind-self · pdf-p3] "Neither reductionist nor mystical, *The Grammar of Viability* reframes soul and spirit as functional realities, diagnoses institutional and cultural failure modes, and offers a disciplined framework for restoring orientation in an era of accelerating constraint. The work does not present a final theory, but a **constraint-faithful lens** capable of integrating knowledge without erasing difference — aimed at preserving coherence where it matters most."
docs/research/connections/sahely-grammar-of-viability.md:89:- **C-10** [anchor: §Executive Summary closing · orn:source:bsahely-2026-02-11-the-grammar-of-viability-mind-self · pdf-p4] "The book concludes that understanding itself can function as an adaptive capacity — if it remains disciplined by constraint, responsive to feedback, and integrated across scales. *The Grammar of Viability* offers not certainty or prediction, but orientation: a way to recognize what cannot be ignored, what must be integrated, and what it means to keep futures open."
docs/research/connections/sahely-grammar-of-viability.md:122:### 4.1 Grammar-shape distinction (load-bearing for the rest)
docs/research/connections/sahely-grammar-of-viability.md:163:Sahely's 2026-02-11 *Grammar of Viability* states the cluster-canonical 7-claim thesis: viability as silent invariant (C-4), feeling as viability-sensing, self-as-interface (C-5), memory-without-a-single-address (C-6), soul/spirit-as-functional (C-9), ethics-where-futures-shared (C-7), collapse-as-delayed-feedback (C-8) — diagnosed against a Chapter-1 constraint-loss narrative (C-12/C-13). Read against Spore's **post-Bundle-α** canon, every resonance lands on **already-resolved** territory:
