docs/research/connections/polycentric-governance.md:114:This failure-mode literature is load-bearing for Spore: any coordination grammar that claims polycentric-governance lineage must carry specified answers to these failure modes, not elide them. The Tier 1b protocol-society note (C7 there) made the same point via Jessop's typology of governance modalities; this cluster widens it with the contemporary empirical polycentric-governance literature.
tmp/canon-critique-lens-c.md:5:The Spore canon is unusually well-grounded compared to most governance grammars. The 26 ADRs and 95 bridge notes in the supporting corpus provide substantive evidence for nearly all load-bearing claims, particularly around control-based coordination failure, reproduction primacy, polycentric governance, and the three-layer coordination stack. However, three structural gaps merit attention: (1) **adopted-surface examples are illustrative, not validated** — the "How Adoption Works" section (project-vision §131–141) sketches incremental reversibility as a design intention but lacks operational evidence from actual adoptions beyond the canonical BKC reference; (2) **the bioregion-as-natural-holon claim is asserted without detailed grounding** in how actual overlapping hydrology, species movement, and indigenous jurisdiction practically manifest as one holon's agency interface; and (3) **Inspirations section cites influential-but-unintegrated frameworks** — Bennett and Friston are named as grounding for intent-pressure and active-inference respectively, but no corpus synthesis shows how their formal machinery would operationalize inside Spore's coordination grammar, leaving the citation more genealogical than structural. These are S2 findings (ungrounded after thorough search) rather than S1 (contradicted by corpus). No findings rose to S1 severity; several corpus elements were already self-aware about their own held-open tensions via ADRs, particularly around reproduction primacy (ADR-0002 acknowledges 48% opposition density explicitly in canon prose).
docs/research/connections/sahely-life-coherent-civilization.md:44:**Caveat (read first):** This is a Spore-side, **framing-note-only**, descriptive comparative-intake artifact (Layer 1.5) for Bichara Sahely's 2026-05-09 publication monograph *"Life-Coherent Civilization: From World-Bringing to Participatory Repair"* (524 pages; AI-co-authored with ChatGPT GPT-5.5 Thinking + NotebookLM; Sahely-authored). It is a **Phase 3 broader-cluster** bridge note, lighter than the Phase 2 anchors. It surfaces resonance against the **resolved** post-Bundle-α Spore canon (concepts-yaml v24; 4 cross-cutting doctrines; 40 canon-decisions through ADR-0090) and proposes **no admission**: the canon-pressure this monograph touches (life-value, civil commons, the great-inversion failure-shape, recursive self-audit) was already dispositioned in the Sahely Bundle α arc (ADRs 0085–0090, admitted 2026-05-23 → 05-29). This note cites that resolved state, not the earlier "pending DECISION-BRIEF" framing. **Single-source rigor:** Sahely is one AI-co-authored author corpus; cross-post recurrence within his corpus is NOT independent evidence and is not summed as convergence here. Cross-repo §5 is descriptive only per stream-scope discipline.
tmp/sahely-corpus-canon-pressure-decision-brief-2026-05-22.md:33:This brief is the Layer-2 (DECISION-BRIEF) artifact in the 3-layer intake-to-vocab-admission program (`feedback_intake_to_vocab_admission_program.md`). Wave 1+2 bridge notes (Layer 1.5) surfaced **20 distinct R-claims with disposition `canon-pressure-DECISION-BRIEF`** across 6 anchor bridge notes. This brief consolidates those R-claims into **14 candidate clusters** (per operator's §1 consolidation guidance), applies honest-rigor cluster-counting per ADR-0064 (`feedback_surface_vocabulary_vs_operational_concern.md`), and surfaces per-candidate disposition recommendations for **operator ratification**.
