tmp/codex-review-spore-identity.out:38:4. **The reframe content itself.** Is *"a coordination grammar … one that holds local sovereignty and global coherence at once, across scales and scopes"* actually more accurate and better than the current *"an infrastructure for collective agency — a common grammar for plural, sovereign coordination across scales and scopes"*? What is LOST by demoting "infrastructure for collective agency" out of the lead (does the agency framing carry something the grammar framing drops)? Critically: is *"holds local sovereignty and global coherence at once"* a claim the canon's OWN universality-discipline (ADR-0031/0032/0044, "at each scale Spore has reached") would flag as overreach — does "at once / across scales and scopes" promise more than a grammar actually delivers?
tmp/codex-review-spore-identity.out:66:    13	Why "Spore"? A spore is portable, generative, and context-sensitive: it moves through larger living networks, lands in a place, and unfolds locally. Spore follows the same logic — a shared coordination grammar that can land across projects, grow in different forms, and remain interoperable without requiring centralization.
tmp/codex-review-spore-identity.out:94:    89	- **Earned (frontstage-ready now):** the *design move* (route through coherence; obstruction-is-information; don't-flatten-sovereignty); the four-layer architecture (it's the existing topology); the two-register discipline; "local-to-global coherence" as plain language for what the grammar already does.
tmp/codex-review-spore-identity.out:143:# Reframing Proposal: Spore Identity — Coordination Grammar
tmp/codex-review-spore-identity.out:152:The current line leads with **"infrastructure for collective agency"** — an *agency-enablement* frame. It gestures at the grammar role in its second clause (*"a common grammar for plural, sovereign coordination"*), but the lead under-names what the work across the repo constellation has revealed Spore actually to be: **the upstream coordination grammar that everything else instantiates** — Layer 1 in the four-layer architecture, the substrate the constellation orients around — the grammar that lets local parts keep their own meaning while still composing into a whole that holds together (local sovereignty and global coherence at once).
tmp/codex-review-spore-identity.out:154:This is not a contradiction the way `reframing-pm-canon-scope` was a contradiction. It is an **identity that the work has outgrown.** The 2026-06-02 repo-constellation strategy work (`~/.claude/plans/so-i-had-this-proud-kahn.md` Track B; `docs/research/synthesis/coherence-without-collapse-and-its-projections.md`) established that Spore is turning out to be upstream of Intelligence Commons, bregion, BKC, bioregional-economics, Poietic Match, *and* the non-canon applications (CIE, the RC financing facility, the GPU node network) — each consuming Spore's grammar. The four-layer integration architecture names Spore as **Layer 1 — Grammar**, the source the rest of the constellation orients around. The identity sentence should name that role plainly, in the register the operator already uses for it externally (sheaf.lol's *"coordination grammar for plural / sovereign / local-to-global coherent agentic systems"*; the Schmidt grant bio's *"shared coordination substrates"*).
tmp/codex-review-spore-identity.out:160:Reframe both identity surfaces toward a **math-quiet "coordination grammar / local-to-global coherence" framing (Option A).** The wording leads with *coordination grammar* (operator lean, 2026-06-02 — the most continuous choice, since "grammar" is already canon's own term and matches "Layer 1 — Grammar" in the architecture) and names *holding local sovereignty and global coherence at once* as what the grammar is for.
tmp/codex-review-spore-identity.out:167:> "Spore is a coordination grammar for plural, sovereign systems — one that holds local sovereignty and global coherence at once, across scales and scopes."
tmp/codex-review-spore-identity.out:170:> "Spore is a coordination grammar for plural, sovereign systems — one that holds local sovereignty and global coherence at once, across scales and scopes: it lets autonomous parts keep their own meaning while still composing into a whole that holds together. As an infrastructure for collective agency, it operates across the normative commitments that shape how agents see, value, know, and act (ontological, axiological, epistemological, practical)…"
tmp/codex-review-spore-identity.out:173:> "Spore follows the same logic — a shared coordination grammar that can land across projects, grow in different forms, and stay coherent across them without requiring centralization."
tmp/codex-review-spore-identity.out:183:5. **`"coordination grammar"` is the chosen lead noun; `"infrastructure for collective agency"` is retained as secondary phrasing, not the lead** (resolved 2026-06-02) — under a grammar-lead the separate *"a common grammar for…"* clause is folded into the lead, and *"infrastructure for collective agency"* moves to a secondary clause in the project-vision paragraph (*"As an infrastructure for collective agency, it operates across the normative commitments…"*). README.md:7 stays lean (tagline only; no secondary clause).
tmp/codex-review-spore-identity.out:187:This change exceeds ordinary canon-review ADR scope under **FR-1 criterion 5 (changes the definition of canon scope), read at the identity layer.** The opening sentence of `project-vision.md` (a `doc_kind: vision` constitutional doc) is the canon's self-definition. Repositioning Spore from *"infrastructure for collective agency"* to *"a coordination grammar (local-to-global coherence)"* changes what the canon says it **is**, and its role relative to the downstream-aligned (IC, PM) and peer-instance-family (bregion, BKC, bioregional-economics) constellation that now treats Spore as upstream. **FR-2 (ambiguity rule)** is explicit: a change that *"alters a layer's identity"* defaults to foundational reframing, *"not downward."*
tmp/codex-review-spore-identity.out:191:**Honest narrowing.** This is the *narrowest* canon-scope sub-case — the self-description wording, not the governed-surface boundary that `reframing-pm-canon-scope` re-drew (which re-layered a doc out of canon). It is therefore lighter than that precedent. It still belongs here because (a) FR-2 routes it here, (b) the operator elected the heavier ceremony for the constitutional identity line, and (c) the cost of getting the one orienting sentence wrong is high: it propagates to the README, sheaf.lol, grant materials, and every downstream *"Spore is…"* citation. The 7-day cooling-off is cheap insurance against a hasty edit to the line the whole constellation reads first.
