docs/research/connections/bosca-ghrist-local-to-global.md:74:- **Paper scope is mathematical, not coordination-theoretic.** The paper treats networks as machines to be analyzed, not as social systems. Translation to Spore's coordination grammar requires additional framing work — the paper supplies algorithms, not coordination primitives.
docs/research/connections/federation-and-interop.md:213:- **R4**: Spore's lexicon entry for stigmergy should acknowledge the federation-protocol-as-stigmergic-substrate reading suggested by the Fediverse and Ramos protocol-commons sources: federation protocols (ActivityPub, polyglot stacks) function stigmergically — actors coordinate by modifying a shared medium (the protocol-level message substrate) rather than through direct communication, and the bee-and-flower asymmetric-reciprocal framing supplies a biological analogy for this that is recognizable to commons-and-ecology audiences. The lexicon entry should state explicitly that stigmergy in Spore's coordination grammar operates across three distinct substrates (technical protocol, institutional rights/semantics, social/organizational practice) consistent with the three-layer interoperability model, so that stigmergic coordination is not read as a technical-only claim. [target: spore.term.stigmergy] [concept: boundary-spanner]
docs/research/connections/sahely-philosophy-and-world-problems.md:33:**Caveat (read first):** This is a Spore-side substrate-resonance bridge note for Bichara Sahely's 2026-02-24 post *"Philosophy and World Problems: Life-Value, Justice and the Civil Commons"* (curated-abstract; ~30 pages; bsahely.com blog post + companion PDF; AI-co-authored with NotebookLM + ChatGPT 5.2). It is a **descriptive, framing-note-only** comparative-intake artifact at Layer 1.5 of the intake program (`feedback_intake_to_vocab_admission_program.md`). It is a **Phase 3 lighter-ceremony** note: the canon-pressure this McMurtry life-value / civil-commons cluster carries was **already adjudicated and resolved** in the Sahely Bundle α arc (ADR-0085 through ADR-0090, landed 2026-05-23 → 05-29). This note therefore **proposes no admission** and surfaces no new canon-pressure; it maps the post's claims onto already-resolved canon and records the cross-reference. Cross-repo §5 is descriptive comparison only per stream-scope discipline (`feedback_workstream_scope_discipline.md`) — no write-side recommendation for IC/bregion/BKC/PM.
docs/research/connections/sahely-self-domestication-of-humans.md:31:**Caveat (read first):** This is a Spore-side substrate-resonance bridge note for Bichara Sahely's 2017-11-09 foundation-era blog post *"The Self-Domestication of Humans"* (bsahely.com, banner *TOWARD LIFE-KNOWLEDGE*). It is a **descriptive, framing-note-only** comparative-intake artifact at Layer 1.5 of the intake program (`feedback_intake_to_vocab_admission_program.md`). It **admits nothing.** **FOUNDATION-ERA framing:** this is a 2017–2018 post, *pre* the Dec-2022+ AI-co-authored white-paper explosion in the Sahely corpus; it is **Sahely-original (pre-AI)** as a *curation act*, but the verbatim body is **third-party editorial/journal text Sahely aggregated** (CARTA symposium blurbs + two journal abstracts), not Sahely's own prose. Per single-source rigor, Sahely is one author corpus and this is one *curation signal* within it; recurrence inside that corpus is **not** independent evidence and is not summed as convergence. Post-Bundle-α canon is cited in its resolved state (concepts-yaml v24; 4 cross-cutting doctrines; 40 canon-decisions through ADR-0090). Cross-repo §5 is descriptive comparison only per stream-scope discipline (`feedback_workstream_scope_discipline.md`); it makes no write-side recommendation.
docs/research/connections/boundary-commoning.md:71:These scopes are not competitors. They are composed: a federation of commons uses within-commons governance per commons AND boundary commoning between commons. Spore should state this explicitly in the field/membrane documentation so the coordination grammar doesn't conflate membership rules with composition rules.
docs/research/connections/boundary-commoning.md:77:This directly rebuts any reading of Spore's collective-agency vocabulary as anti-governance. A coordination grammar that claims commons lineage must carry the operational surface the tradition specifies — or explicitly say it is doing something else.
docs/research/connections/digital-labor-peer-production-opposition.md:111:Kleiner's core argument — "if commons-based peer-production is limited exclusively to a commons made of digital property with virtually no reproduction costs, how can the use-value produced be translated into exchange-value? Where is the money to pay for the production of these valuable things? … But if what they produce has no exchange-value, how can the peer producers be able to acquire the material needs for their own subsistence?" — establishes that peer-production discourse restricted to non-rivalrous information commons cannot account for participant subsistence, and that any coordination grammar invoking peer-production or knowledge-commons vocabulary without specifying how participants draw material subsistence is structurally incomplete under the peer-production tradition's own internal critique.
