docs/research/corpus-review/originals/sahely-extractions/2026-04-10-the-architecture-of-viability-navigating-complex-systems-from-relational.md:101:11. **Architecture of Viability as 531pp summit predecessor (cluster relationship)** — This 126pp paper is the EARLIER framing of the doctrine Sahely later expanded to the 531pp Wave-2 anchor #6 (Apr 27, "Architecture of Viability: A Grammar of Coherence for Life, Mind, Society, and Cosmos"). Wave-2 #6 EXTENDS Wave-9 #5 by scoping the Fano/E7/Albert framework from systems-medicine/governance/infrastructure to additionally include life / mind / society / cosmos layers. Wave-9 #5 carries the core mathematical lift; Wave-2 #6 carries the full philosophical / cross-scale expansion.
tmp/graphiti-poc/build/graphiti/tests/evals/data/longmemeval_data/longmemeval_oracle.json:7955:                    "content": "As a Spanish speaker, you'll find that there are many similarities and cognates between Spanish and French, as well as some similarities with German. This is because all three languages share a common Latin root and have borrowed words from each other throughout history.\n\n**Spanish-French Cognates:**\n\n1. **Vocabulary:** Many words are identical or very similar in both languages, such as:\n\t* familia (Spanish) = famille (French)\n\t* historia (Spanish) = histoire (French)\n\t* telefono (Spanish) = t\u00e9l\u00e9phone (French)\n\t* universidad (Spanish) = universit\u00e9 (French)\n2. **Grammar:** French and Spanish share similar sentence structures and verb conjugations, such as:\n\t* Verb endings: -ar, -er, -ir (e.g., hablar, comer, vivir in Spanish; parler, manger, vivre in French)\n\t* Use of subjunctive mood in similar contexts\n3. **Pronunciation:** French and Spanish share similar sounds and intonation patterns, making it easier for Spanish speakers to pick up French pronunciation.\n\n**Spanish-German Cognates:**\n\n1. **Vocabulary:** Although German is a Germanic language, it has borrowed many words from Latin and French, making it easier for Spanish speakers to recognize:\n\t* familia (Spanish) = Familie (German)\n\t* historia (Spanish) = Geschichte (German)\n\t* universidad (Spanish) = Universit\u00e4t (German)\n2. **Cognates in loanwords:** Many German words have been borrowed from French or Latin, making them similar to Spanish words:\n\t* B\u00fcro (German) = oficina (Spanish) = bureau (French)\n\t* Hotel (German) = hotel (Spanish) = h\u00f4tel (French)\n3. **Grammar:** German and Spanish share some grammatical structures, such as:\n\t* Use of verb conjugation suffixes (e.g., -ar, -er, -ir in Spanish; -en, -eln, -ern in German)\n\n**Tips for Leveraging Cognates:**\n\n1. **Focus on similarities:** Identify words and grammar structures that are similar between Spanish and French or German, and build upon those.\n2. **Use flashcards:** Create flashcards with words in Spanish and their French or German equivalents to help you memorize cognates.\n3. **Listen to native speakers:** Listen to native speakers of French and German to get a feel for the pronunciation and intonation patterns.\n4. **Practice, practice, practice:** The more you practice speaking, writing, and listening to French and German, the more you'll notice the similarities and cognates.\n\nBy leveraging these similarities and cognates, you'll be able to learn French and German more quickly and efficiently. Good luck, and have fun exploring these new languages!",
tmp/graphiti-poc/build/graphiti/tests/evals/data/longmemeval_data/longmemeval_oracle.json:15997:                    "content": "You're on the right track! Focusing on high-protein, high-fiber meals with minimal processed foods and added sugars will definitely support your fitness goals. \n\nI'm more than happy to provide you with some delicious and affordable protein-rich recipe ideas that are easy to make and perfect for meal prep. Here are some options:\n\n**Breakfast Recipes:**\n\n1. **Protein-Packed Omelette**: Whisk 2 eggs, 1/4 cup cottage cheese, and 1 tablespoon chopped spinach. Cook in a non-stick pan with 1 tablespoon olive oil. Fold in 1/4 cup sliced bell peppers and onions. ($1.50 per serving)\n2. **Avocado Toast with Scrambled Eggs**: Toast whole grain bread, mash 1/2 avocado, and top with 2 scrambled eggs. Add salt, pepper, and a squeeze of lemon juice. ($2.50 per serving)\n3. **Greek Yogurt Parfait**: Layer 1 cup Greek yogurt, 1/2 cup mixed berries, 1/4 cup granola, and 1 scoop whey protein powder in a jar. ($2.50 per serving)\n\n**Lunch Recipes:**\n\n1. **Grilled Chicken Breast with Quinoa and Veggies**: Marinate 4 oz chicken breast in 1 tablespoon olive oil, 1 tablespoon lemon juice, and 1 clove garlic. Grill and serve with 1/2 cup cooked quinoa and 1 cup steamed broccoli. ($3.50 per serving)\n2. **Lentil Soup**: Cook 1 cup lentils, 1 onion, 2 cloves garlic, and 1 carrot in 4 cups vegetable broth. Add 1 can diced tomatoes and 1 teaspoon cumin. Serve with 1 slice whole grain bread. ($2.50 per serving)\n3. **Turkey and Avocado Wrap**: Spread 1 tablespoon hummus on a whole grain tortilla, layer with 2 oz sliced turkey breast, 1/2 avocado, 1 cup mixed greens, and 1/4 cup sliced cucumber. Roll up and slice in half. ($3.50 per serving)\n\n**Dinner Recipes:**\n\n1. **Baked Salmon with Sweet Potatoes and Green Beans**: Season 4 oz salmon fillet with salt, pepper, and 1 tablespoon olive oil. Bake with 1 medium sweet potato and 1 cup green beans at 400\u00b0F (200\u00b0C) for 20-25 minutes. ($4.50 per serving)\n2. **Chicken and Veggie Stir-Fry**: Cook 4 oz chicken breast, 1 cup mixed veggies (bell peppers, onions, broccoli), and 2 cloves garlic in 1 tablespoon olive oil. Serve with 1/2 cup cooked brown rice. ($3.50 per serving)\n3. **Black Bean and Chicken Chili**: Cook 1 cup black beans, 4 oz chicken breast, 1 onion, and 2 cloves garlic in 2 cups vegetable broth. Add 1 can diced tomatoes and 1 teaspoon cumin. Serve with 1 slice whole grain bread. ($3.50 per serving)\n\n**Snack Recipes:**\n\n1. **Greek Yogurt with Berries and Nuts**: Mix 1 cup Greek yogurt, 1/2 cup mixed berries, and 1 tablespoon chopped nuts. ($2.50 per serving)\n2. **Hard-Boiled Eggs**: Boil 6 eggs and season with salt and pepper. ($1.50 per serving)\n3. **Cottage Cheese with Cucumber Slices**: Mix 1/2 cup cottage cheese with 1/4 cup sliced cucumber and 1 tablespoon chopped parsley. ($2.50 per serving)\n\nThese recipes are not only delicious but also packed with protein, fiber, and nutrients to support your fitness goals. Plus, they're easy on the wallet!\n\nRemember to adjust the portion",
tmp/graphiti-poc/build/graphiti/tests/evals/data/longmemeval_data/longmemeval_oracle.json:19646:                    "content": "It sounds like you've already taken a great step by attending a writing workshop and getting feedback from a published author! Now, to help you further, I'd be happy to provide you with some resources on character development and editing.\n\n**Character Development:**\n\n1. **Character Arcs:** A crucial aspect of character development is creating a compelling character arc. Check out this article by K.M. Weiland on \"The 5 Key Elements of a Character Arc\" to learn more.\n2. **Character Interviews:** This technique can help you get into your character's head and flesh out their personality, motivations, and backstory. You can find a character interview template online or create your own questions.\n3. **Character Profiles:** Develop detailed profiles for your main characters, including their physical appearance, personality traits, strengths, weaknesses, goals, and motivations. This will help you keep track of their development throughout the story.\n4. **Character-driven Plotting:** Focus on how your characters' actions and decisions drive the plot forward. This article by Janice Hardy on \"How to Create a Character-Driven Plot\" can provide valuable insights.\n\n**Editing:**\n\n1. **Self-Editing Checklist:** Create a checklist of common mistakes to watch out for, such as grammar errors, pacing issues, and inconsistent character traits. You can find many self-editing checklists online or create your own based on your workshop feedback.\n2. **Beta Readers:** Share your work with beta readers who can provide feedback on character development, pacing, and overall story coherence. You can find beta readers through writing communities, online forums, or by joining a writing group.\n3. **Revise and Revise Again:** Editing is a crucial step in the writing process. Be prepared to make significant changes to your manuscript to strengthen your story and characters.\n4. **Editing Resources:** Check out resources like Grammar Girl, ProWritingAid, or AutoCrit to help with grammar, sentence structure, and writing style.\n\n**Additional Resources:**\n\n1. **Writing Books:** \"The Writer's Journey: Mythic Structure for Writers\" by Christopher Vogler, \"Character & Viewpoint\" by Orson Scott Card, and \"Self-Editing for Fiction Writers\" by Renni Browne and Dave King are excellent resources for writers.\n2. **Online Writing Communities:** Join online forums like Writing.com, NaNoWriMo, or Reddit's r/writingcommunity to connect with fellow writers, get feedback, and learn from others.\n3. **Writing Podcasts:** Listen to podcasts like \"The Creative Penn,\" \"I Should Be Writing,\" or \"The Portfolio Life\" for tips, inspiration, and industry insights.\n\nRemember, writing is a process that takes time, effort, and practice. Don't be afraid to make mistakes or try new techniques. Keep writing, and most importantly, have fun!\n\nDo you have any specific questions about character development or editing that I can help with?",
tmp/graphiti-poc/build/graphiti/tests/evals/data/longmemeval_data/longmemeval_oracle.json:19708:                    "content": "Optimizing your website for search engines is a crucial aspect of digital marketing. I'm glad you learned a lot from the workshop!\n\nThere are many excellent SEO tools available, ranging from free to premium, that can help you improve your website's visibility and ranking on search engines. Here are some popular ones:\n\n**Keyword Research Tools:**\n\n1. **Google Keyword Planner** (free): A great starting point for keyword research, offering insights into search volume, competition, and suggested bid prices.\n2. **Ahrefs** (paid): A comprehensive tool for keyword research, backlink analysis, and content optimization.\n3. **SEMrush** (paid): Offers keyword research, competitor analysis, and technical SEO audits.\n4. **Moz Keyword Explorer** (paid): A keyword research tool that provides insights into search volume, competition, and priority scores.\n\n**Technical SEO Tools:**\n\n1. **Google Search Console** (free): A must-have tool for monitoring website performance, search analytics, and technical SEO issues.\n2. **Google PageSpeed Insights** (free): Analyzes your website's loading speed and provides recommendations for improvement.\n3. **Screaming Frog SEO Spider** (free/paid): A crawling tool that identifies technical SEO issues, such as broken links and duplicate content.\n4. **GTmetrix** (free/paid): Analyzes website performance, providing insights into page speed, loading times, and optimization opportunities.\n\n**Backlink Analysis Tools:**\n\n1. **Ahrefs** (paid): Offers a comprehensive backlink analysis, including anchor text distribution, referring domains, and link quality.\n2. **SEMrush** (paid): Provides backlink analysis, competitor research, and technical SEO audits.\n3. **Moz** (paid): Offers backlink analysis, including link equity, anchor text distribution, and spam score.\n4. **OpenLinkProfiler** (free): A free backlink analysis tool that provides insights into linking domains, anchor texts, and link quality.\n\n**Content Optimization Tools:**\n\n1. **Yoast SEO** (free/paid): A popular WordPress plugin that helps with on-page optimization, including keyword usage, meta tags, and content readability.\n2. **SEMrush** (paid): Offers content optimization tools, including content analysis, keyword suggestions, and readability scoring.\n3. **Grammarly** (free/paid): A writing tool that helps with grammar, spelling, and readability, which can indirectly improve SEO.\n4. **Hemingway Editor** (free): A simple tool that simplifies your content, making it more readable and SEO-friendly.\n\nThese are just a few examples of the many SEO tools available. You may want to explore each option to see which ones fit your needs and budget.\n\nRemember, SEO is an ongoing process that requires regular monitoring, optimization, and improvement. By using these tools, you'll be well on your way to enhancing your website's search engine ranking and driving more organic traffic to your site.",
tmp/graphiti-poc/build/graphiti/tests/evals/data/longmemeval_data/longmemeval_oracle.json:23672:                    "content": "Editing and proofreading are crucial steps in the report-writing process, and it's great that you want to ensure your report is error-free and polished.\n\nHere are some tips to help you make the editing and proofreading process more efficient on Friday:\n\n1. **Use a checklist:** Create a checklist of common errors to look out for, such as grammar, punctuation, formatting, and consistency. This will help you stay focused and ensure you don't miss anything.\n2. **Edit in passes:** Break down the editing process into smaller passes, focusing on one aspect at a time. For example, do a pass for grammar and punctuation, followed by a pass for clarity and coherence, and finally a pass for formatting and consistency.\n3. **Use technology to your advantage:** Utilize tools like grammar and spell checkers, as well as plugins like Grammarly or ProWritingAid, to help identify errors and suggest improvements.\n4. **Read aloud:** Read your report aloud to catch any awkward phrasing, unclear sentences, or typos that your eyes might miss.\n5. **Get a second pair of eyes:** If possible, ask a colleague or friend to review your report and provide feedback. A fresh perspective can help catch errors or suggest improvements you might have missed.\n6. **Prioritize the most critical sections:** Focus on editing and proofreading the most critical sections of the report, such as the executive summary, introduction, and conclusion, before moving on to the rest of the document.\n7. **Use a timer:** Set a timer for a specific amount of time, say 25 minutes, and edit as much as you can during that time. Take a short break, and then repeat the process. This technique is called the Pomodoro Technique.\n8. **Review your report in a different environment:** Sometimes, a change of scenery can help you approach your report with a fresh perspective. Try reviewing your report in a different location, or on a different device, to catch any errors or areas for improvement.\n\nBy implementing these strategies, you can make the editing and proofreading process more efficient and effective, even on a tight deadline.\n\nAdditionally, consider allocating a bit more time for editing and proofreading on Friday, or adjusting your schedule slightly to ensure you have enough time to complete these tasks to your satisfaction.\n\nHow do these tips sound? Do you have any other questions or concerns about the editing and proofreading process?",
tmp/graphiti-poc/build/graphiti/tests/evals/data/longmemeval_data/longmemeval_oracle.json:38378:                    "content": "Sure, here are 100 prompt parameters that you can specify to influence my output:\n\n1. Voice (e.g., male or female)\n2. Tone (e.g., serious, sarcastic, humorous, etc.)\n3. Register (e.g., formal, informal, academic, conversational, etc.)\n4. Style (e.g., narrative, descriptive, expository, persuasive, etc.)\n5. Audience (e.g., children, adults, experts, non-experts, etc.)\n6. Genre (e.g., fiction, non-fiction, poetry, drama, etc.)\n7. Language (e.g., English, French, Spanish, etc.)\n8. Dialect (e.g., American English, British English, etc.)\n9. Purpose (e.g., informative, instructive, entertaining, etc.)\n10. Mood (e.g., happy, sad, angry, etc.)\n11. Point of view (e.g., first person, third person, etc.)\n12. Setting (e.g., time period, location, etc.)\n13. Characterization (e.g., flat, round, static, dynamic, etc.)\n14. Conflict (e.g., internal, external, etc.)\n15. Plot (e.g., exposition, rising action, climax, etc.)\n16. Theme (e.g., love, friendship, betrayal, etc.)\n17. Symbolism (e.g., colors, objects, etc.)\n18. Imagery (e.g., visual, auditory, olfactory, etc.)\n19. Figurative language (e.g., simile, metaphor, hyperbole, etc.)\n20. Allusion (e.g., historical, cultural, literary, etc.)\n21. Irony (e.g., verbal, situational, dramatic, etc.)\n22. Foreshadowing (e.g., subtle, explicit, etc.)\n23. Flashback (e.g., chronological, non-chronological, etc.)\n24. Dialogue (e.g., realistic, stylized, etc.)\n25. Monologue (e.g., internal, external, etc.)\n26. Soliloquy (e.g., dramatic, introspective, etc.)\n27. Sound effects (e.g., ambient, diegetic, non-diegetic, etc.)\n28. Music (e.g., genre, tempo, etc.)\n29. Visual effects (e.g., lighting, color, etc.)\n30. Camera angles (e.g., low, high, close-up, etc.)\n31. Shot composition (e.g., rule of thirds, symmetry, etc.)\n32. Editing (e.g., cuts, fades, transitions, etc.)\n33. Pacing (e.g., fast, slow, steady, etc.)\n34. Structure (e.g., chronological, non-chronological, etc.)\n35. Tone of voice (e.g., serious, angry, sad, etc.)\n36. Accent (e.g., regional, international, etc.)\n37. Syntax (e.g., simple, complex, compound, etc.)\n38. Vocabulary (e.g., formal, informal, technical, etc.)\n39. Grammar (e.g., correct, incorrect, etc.)\n40. Spelling (e.g., American, British, etc.)\n41. Pronunciation (e.g., clear, mumbled, etc.)\n42. Enunciation (e.g., precise, slurred, etc.)\n43. Volume (e.g., loud, soft, etc.)\n44. Inflection (e.g., rising, falling, etc.)\n45. Pitch (e.g., high, low, etc.)\n46. Speed (e.g., fast, slow, etc.)\n47. Diction (e.g., clear, mumbled, etc.)\n48. Rhythm (e.g., staccato, legato, etc.)\n49. Timing (",
docs/research/corpus-review/originals/sahely-extractions/2026-05-05-from-the-biology-of-love-to-life-coherent-governance-maturanan-galtungian-mcmurtrian.md:33:**Cluster role**: **WAVE-11 SECONDARY ANCHOR (9-fact hand-curated)**. **First explicit cross-substrate Sahely synthesis** of three primary traditions: Maturana (biology of love) + Galtung (direct/structural/cultural violence) + McMurtry (life-value onto-axiology, civil commons). Bridges Wave-7's Galtung-McMurtry violence-cluster (Feb 2026) into the May-arc Maturanan-substrate. Where Wave-7 anchor #6 (Grammar of Violence) developed the Galtung-McMurtry pair, this paper integrates them with Maturana's biology of love as common ground. Also integrates Elinor Ostrom (commons governance) as fourth substrate, completing the four-thinker synthesis. Part of the **May-5 triple-track** (#5 emotioning + #6 evolutionary + #7 governance) — three Maturanan-framework variations published same day.
docs/research/corpus-review/originals/sahely-extractions/2026-05-05-from-the-biology-of-love-to-life-coherent-governance-maturanan-galtungian-mcmurtrian.md:61:   - Final formulation: "**Peace is not merely the absence of violence; it is the organized presence of life-enabling conditions.**" Cites Galtung 1969 (Violence, Peace, and Peace Research) + Galtung 1990 (Cultural Violence). **Connects to Wave-7 anchor #6 Grammar of Violence**: this paper translates Wave-7's Galtung-McMurtry violence-substrate into positive life-coherence-substrate. Cross-cluster bridge.
docs/research/corpus-review/originals/sahely-extractions/2026-05-05-from-the-biology-of-love-to-life-coherent-governance-maturanan-galtungian-mcmurtrian.md:94:- **IN_CLUSTER_WITH Wave-7 anchor #6 (Grammar of Violence)** — cross-cluster bridge: this paper integrates Wave-7's Galtung-McMurtry violence-substrate with Wave-11's Maturanan-substrate; first explicit triple-thinker synthesis in Sahely corpus
